window.TOPICS=window.TOPICS||{};window.TOPICS['kernels']={"id": "kernels", "title": "Kernels and Kernel PCA", "tagline": "Feature maps, valid kernels, polynomial and Gaussian, kernel PCA. 22 PYQs (78.5 marks) solved step by step.", "concepts": {"phi": {"name": "Feature maps and kernels", "marks": 15, "ease": 2, "repair": "<p>Expand the kernel until it is a plain sum. Each term must split into (something with only $x$) times (the same thing with only $y$); that something is one feature. A number $c$ in front becomes $\\sqrt c$ on both sides.</p>"}, "poly": {"name": "Polynomial kernels", "marks": 7, "ease": 3, "repair": "<p>Three moves, in order: dot product, add the constant, raise to the power. To count features use $\\binom{d+m}{m}$ when there is a $+1$, and $\\binom{d+m-1}{m}$ without it.</p>"}, "gauss": {"name": "Gaussian kernel", "marks": 3, "ease": 3, "repair": "<p>Squared distance first, divide by $2\\sigma^2$, put a minus sign, exponentiate. The answer is always between $0$ and $1$, and exactly $1$ for identical points.</p>"}, "valid": {"name": "Valid kernels", "marks": 34.5, "ease": 1, "repair": "<p>Try to break it before trying to prove it: check $k(x,x)\\ge0$ at simple points like $x=0$, then symmetry, then a two-point Gram matrix. If nothing breaks, build it from known kernels using sums with non-negative weights, products and $f(x)kf(y)$.</p>"}, "kpca": {"name": "Kernel PCA", "marks": 19, "ease": 1, "repair": "<p>Kernel PCA is dual PCA with kernel values in the table. The one extra step is centring: subtract row and column means and add back the grand mean. Then eigenvalues divided by $n$ are variances, and there are at most $n-1$ non-zero ones.</p>"}}, "sections": [{"id": "orient", "title": "Orientation: where kernels fit and what you will learn", "html": "<p>Kernels answer one question: <b>how can a method that only uses straight lines handle data that is not straight?</b> The idea is to move every point into a larger space where the problem becomes straight, and to do that move cheaply, without ever writing the larger space down.</p><div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"bt\">Where this sits</div><p>Before this: <b>PCA</b> (directions of largest variance) and <b>dual PCA</b>, which looks at the data only through the table of dot products $x_i^Tx_j$. After this: <b>kernel PCA</b>, which replaces that table by a kernel table, and the legacy SVM topic.</p></div><h3>What you will be able to do</h3><ol><li>Read a feature map $\\phi$ off a kernel (and the reverse: compute a kernel from $\\phi$).</li><li>Evaluate polynomial and Gaussian kernels, and count polynomial features.</li><li>Decide whether a formula is a valid kernel, quickly and with a reason.</li><li>Centre a Gram matrix and read variance and component counts for kernel PCA.</li></ol><h3>Prerequisite check (do these first)</h3><details class=\"rc\"><summary>Dot product: $(1,2)^T(3,1)=?$</summary><p>$1\\cdot3+2\\cdot1=5$. The dot product is symmetric: $x^Ty=y^Tx$, and $x^Tx=\\|x\\|^2\\ge0$.</p></details><details class=\"rc\"><summary>Expand $(x_1y_1+x_2y_2)^2$</summary><p>$=x_1^2y_1^2+x_2^2y_2^2+2x_1x_2y_1y_2$. Three terms; the middle one carries a factor $2$.</p></details><details class=\"rc\"><summary>Binomial coefficient: $\\binom72$ and $\\binom73$</summary><p>$\\binom nk=\\dfrac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}$, so $\\binom72=21$ and $\\binom73=35$.</p></details><details class=\"rc\"><summary>Gram matrix of $x_1=(1,2)$, $x_2=(2,1)$</summary><p>$K_{ij}=x_i^Tx_j$: $K_{11}=5$, $K_{22}=5$, $K_{12}=4$, so $K=\\begin{bmatrix}5&4\\\\4&5\\end{bmatrix}$, eigenvalues $9$ and $1$ (both $\\ge0$).</p></details><h3>PYQ sources</h3><p>Converted from the Quiz 1 Kernels notes. The teaching (intuition, widgets, worked examples) stays on these Quiz 1 pages; every PYQ below is solved here in the Quiz 2 format with each option explained.</p><ul><li><a href=\"#q1-b13\">Prerequisites, 3.1 feature maps, 3.3 polynomial and Gaussian</a></li><li><a href=\"#q1-b14\">3.2 Valid kernels (A)</a></li><li><a href=\"#q1-b15\">3.2 Valid kernels (B)</a></li><li><a href=\"#q1-b16\">3.4 Kernel PCA</a></li><li><a href=\"#q1-rev3\">T3 revision and cheat sheet</a></li></ul><p>Questions tagged [FLAG] or [?] have no key in the source or a stem that is partly missing; the reading used is stated.</p>"}, {"id": "phi", "title": "3.1 Feature maps: lifting data so a line can separate it", "html": "<h3>Intuition</h3><p>Take 1-D data: positive points at $x=-1,0,1$ and negative points at $x=-3,-2,2,3$. No single threshold on the line separates them, because the positives sit in the middle. Now give every point a second coordinate, its square: $\\phi(x)=(x,x^2)$. The positives have $x^2\\le1$ and the negatives have $x^2\\ge4$, so the horizontal line $x^2=2.5$ separates them.</p><figure><svg viewBox=\"0 0 360 190\" style=\"width:360px;max-width:100%\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"lifting 1-D points to a parabola\"><line x1=\"20\" y1=\"165\" x2=\"340\" y2=\"165\" stroke=\"currentColor\" opacity=\".4\"/><text x=\"20\" y=\"182\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\">x</text><circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"165\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#e76f51\"/><circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"165\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#e76f51\"/><circle cx=\"140\" cy=\"165\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#2a9d8f\"/><circle cx=\"180\" cy=\"165\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#2a9d8f\"/><circle cx=\"220\" cy=\"165\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#2a9d8f\"/><circle cx=\"260\" cy=\"165\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#e76f51\"/><circle cx=\"300\" cy=\"165\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#e76f51\"/><path d=\"M 60 25 Q 180 305 300 25\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" opacity=\".35\"/><circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"45\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#e76f51\"/><circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"95\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#e76f51\"/><circle cx=\"140\" cy=\"125\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#2a9d8f\"/><circle cx=\"180\" cy=\"135\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#2a9d8f\"/><circle cx=\"220\" cy=\"125\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#2a9d8f\"/><circle cx=\"260\" cy=\"95\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#e76f51\"/><circle cx=\"300\" cy=\"45\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#e76f51\"/><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"320\" y2=\"110\" stroke=\"#264653\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\"/><text x=\"250\" y=\"104\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\">x² = 2.5</text></svg><figcaption>Green points (x = -1, 0, 1) cannot be separated from orange ones on the line. After the lift x ↦ (x, x²) one horizontal line splits them.</figcaption></figure><div class=\"box analogy\"><div class=\"bt\">Everyday analogy</div><p>Pins stuck on a flat map cannot be separated by a straight cut if one colour is surrounded by the other. Lift each pin up on a stick whose height depends on its distance from the centre: now a flat sheet slid between the two heights separates them. <b>Where the analogy stops:</b> a real feature map may lift into hundreds, or infinitely many, dimensions, not just one extra one.</p></div><div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"bt\">Definition: feature map and kernel</div><p>A <b>feature map</b> is a function $\\phi:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\mathbb R^D$ that turns an input $x$ with $d$ numbers into a feature vector with $D$ numbers (usually $D>d$, sometimes $D=\\infty$). The <b>kernel</b> of $\\phi$ is $$k(x,y)=\\phi(x)^T\\phi(y).$$ Symbols: $x,y$ are two inputs; $\\phi(x)^T\\phi(y)$ is the dot product of their feature vectors. The kernel measures how similar two points look after the lift.</p></div><h3>Why it matters: the kernel trick</h3><p>Computing $\\phi(x)^T\\phi(y)$ directly can be expensive (huge $D$). But for many $\\phi$ the dot product collapses to a short formula in $x^Ty$. You then never build $\\phi$: you only evaluate $k$. Example: for $x,y\\in\\mathbb R^2$ with $\\phi(x)=(x_1^2,\\,x_2^2,\\,\\sqrt2x_1x_2)$ we get $\\phi(x)^T\\phi(y)=(x^Ty)^2$.</p><h3>Recipe: from a kernel to a feature map</h3><ol><li>Expand the kernel completely into a sum of terms.</li><li>Group every term as (a function of $x$) $\\times$ (the same function of $y$).</li><li>Each such pair is one coordinate of $\\phi$. If a term has a number $c$ in front, put $\\sqrt c$ into the coordinate for $x$ and $\\sqrt c$ for $y$ (so that $\\sqrt c\\cdot\\sqrt c=c$).</li></ol><div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example 1 (simple)</div><p>$\\phi(x)=(x,x^2)$ for scalars. For $x=2$, $y=3$: $k=\\phi(2)^T\\phi(3)=(2,4)\\cdot(3,9)=6+36=42$. Check by the recipe: $k(x,y)=xy+x^2y^2$, $2\\cdot3+4\\cdot9=42$ ✓.</p></div><div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example 2 (exam level): $k(x,y)=(x^Ty)^2+1$ on $\\mathbb R^2$</div><p>Expand: $(x_1y_1+x_2y_2)^2+1=x_1^2y_1^2+x_2^2y_2^2+2x_1x_2y_1y_2+1$. Pair the terms: $x_1^2\\cdot y_1^2$, $x_2^2\\cdot y_2^2$, $2\\,(x_1x_2)(y_1y_2)=(\\sqrt2x_1x_2)(\\sqrt2y_1y_2)$, and $1\\cdot1$.</p><p>So $$\\phi(x)=\\big(x_1^2,\\ x_2^2,\\ \\sqrt2\\,x_1x_2,\\ 1\\big).$$ Dimension $D=4$. Code-checked on 100 random pairs.</p></div><div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example 3 (tricky): the map is not unique</div><p>Reorder the coordinates, flip signs of coordinates, or apply any rotation $Q$ ($Q^TQ=I$): $(Q\\phi(x))^T(Q\\phi(y))=\\phi(x)^TQ^TQ\\phi(y)=\\phi(x)^T\\phi(y)$. So several options in a multiple-choice question can be correct maps. Look for the <b>weights</b>: a missing $\\sqrt2$ makes the cross term come out as $1$ instead of $2$, and that option is wrong.</p></div><div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"bt\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Forgetting the $\\sqrt2$ on cross terms (writing $x_1x_2$ instead of $\\sqrt2x_1x_2$).</li><li>Forgetting the constant coordinate (the $+1$ in the kernel is a coordinate equal to $1$).</li><li>Assuming $\\phi$ is unique. It is not; only the kernel is.</li><li>Confusing the feature dimension $D$ with the input dimension $d$.</li></ul></div>", "quiz": [{"id": "kq1", "concept": "phi", "level": "easy", "type": "mcq", "stem": "Scalars $x,y$; $k(x,y)=xy+1$. A feature map is", "options": [{"t": "$\\phi(x)=(x,1)$", "ok": true, "why": "Correct: $(x,1)\\cdot(y,1)=xy+1$."}, {"t": "$\\phi(x)=x+1$", "ok": false, "why": "Gives $(x+1)(y+1)$."}, {"t": "$\\phi(x)=(x,x)$", "ok": false, "why": "Gives $2xy$."}, {"t": "$\\phi(x)=(1,1)$", "ok": false, "why": "Gives $2$."}]}, {"id": "kq2", "concept": "phi", "level": "easy", "type": "num", "stem": "$\\phi(x)=(x,x^2)$. Value of $k(1,2)$?", "num": {"ans": 6, "tol": 0.001, "show": "6"}, "explain": "$(1,1)\\cdot(2,4)=2+4=6$.", "hint": ""}, {"id": "kq3", "concept": "phi", "level": "medium", "type": "num", "stem": "Dimension of the feature map for $k(x,y)=(x^Ty)^2+1$, $x\\in\\mathbb R^2$?", "num": {"ans": 4, "tol": 0.001, "show": "4"}, "explain": "$(x_1^2,x_2^2,\\sqrt2x_1x_2,1)$.", "hint": ""}]}, {"id": "poly", "title": "3.3 Polynomial and Gaussian kernels", "html": "<h3>Polynomial kernel</h3><div class=\"box formula\"><div class=\"bt\">Formula</div>$$k(x,y)=(x^Ty+c)^m,\\qquad c\\ge0,\\ m\\in\\{1,2,3,\\dots\\}$$<p>$x^Ty$ is the ordinary dot product, $m$ the degree, $c$ a constant (the exams use $c=1$ unless the stem says otherwise).</p></div><p><b>Number of features.</b> With $c=1$ the feature map contains every monomial of degree up to $m$ in $d$ variables, so $D=\\binom{d+m}{m}$ (this includes the constant $1$). With $c=0$ (homogeneous) only degree exactly $m$ appears, so $D=\\binom{d+m-1}{m}$.</p><div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example 4: how many features?</div><p>Degree-3 polynomial kernel $(1+x^Ty)^3$ with input dimension $d=4$: $D=\\binom{4+3}{3}=\\binom73=35$. Homogeneous $(x^Ty)^3$ with $d=4$: $\\binom{6}{3}=20$. For $d=20$, $m=3$ the full count is $\\binom{23}{3}=1771$.</p></div><div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example 5: evaluate a polynomial kernel</div><p>$k(x,y)=(x^Ty+1)^4$ at $x=(1,-1)$, $y=(2,1)$. Step 1: $x^Ty=1\\cdot2+(-1)\\cdot1=1$. Step 2: add $1$: $2$. Step 3: power $4$: $2^4=16$.</p></div><div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example 6: for $d=2$, $m=2$, $c=1$</div><p>$(1+x_1y_1+x_2y_2)^2$ expands to $1+x_1^2y_1^2+x_2^2y_2^2+2x_1y_1+2x_2y_2+2x_1x_2y_1y_2$, so $\\phi(x)=(1,\\sqrt2x_1,\\sqrt2x_2,x_1^2,x_2^2,\\sqrt2x_1x_2)$, $D=6=\\binom42$ ✓.</p></div><h3>Gaussian kernel</h3><div class=\"box formula\"><div class=\"bt\">Formula</div>$$k(x,y)=\\exp\\!\\Big(-\\frac{\\|x-y\\|^2}{2\\sigma^2}\\Big),\\qquad \\sigma>0$$<p>$\\|x-y\\|^2$ is the squared distance, $\\sigma$ the width. The value is in $(0,1]$: it is $1$ when $x=y$ and shrinks towards $0$ as the points move apart.</p></div><p>Its feature map has infinitely many coordinates, so it can never be written down: this is where the kernel trick is essential. Because $k(x,x)=1$, every point maps to a vector of length $1$.</p><div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example 7: evaluate</div><p>$\\sigma=1$, $x=(0,1)$, $y=(1,0)$. $\\|x-y\\|^2=1+1=2$, so $k=e^{-2/2}=e^{-1}=0.3679$.</p></div><div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example 8 (tricky): the effect of $\\sigma$</div><p>Two points at distance $1$. $\\sigma=0.5$: $e^{-1/(2\\cdot0.25)}=e^{-2}=0.1353$. $\\sigma=1$: $e^{-0.5}=0.6065$. $\\sigma=2$: $e^{-1/8}=0.8825$. Small $\\sigma$ means only very close points count as similar; large $\\sigma$ makes everything look similar.</p><p>Distance in feature space: $\\|\\phi(x)-\\phi(y)\\|^2=k(x,x)+k(y,y)-2k(x,y)$. For the pair above at $\\sigma=1$ this is $2-2(0.6065)=0.787$.</p></div><div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"bt\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Dropping the $+c$ before taking the power, or applying the power to $x^Ty$ only.</li><li>Using $\\|x-y\\|$ instead of its square in the Gaussian.</li><li>Forgetting the $2$ in $2\\sigma^2$.</li><li>Counting features without the constant term (that gives $\\binom{d+m}{m}-1$).</li></ul></div>", "quiz": [{"id": "kq4", "concept": "poly", "level": "easy", "type": "num", "stem": "Features of $(1+x^Ty)^2$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R^3$?", "num": {"ans": 10, "tol": 0.001, "show": "10"}, "explain": "$\\binom52=10$.", "hint": ""}, {"id": "kq5", "concept": "gauss", "level": "easy", "type": "num", "stem": "Gaussian, $\\sigma=1$, $\\|x-y\\|^2=4$. Kernel value (3 decimals)?", "num": {"ans": 0.135, "tol": 0.002, "show": "0.135"}, "explain": "$e^{-4/2}=e^{-2}$.", "hint": ""}, {"id": "kq6", "concept": "gauss", "level": "medium", "type": "mcq", "stem": "For the Gaussian kernel, $k(x,x)$ is", "options": [{"t": "always $1$", "ok": true, "why": "Correct: distance $0$, $e^0=1$."}, {"t": "$\\|x\\|^2$", "ok": false, "why": "That is the linear kernel."}, {"t": "$0$", "ok": false, "why": "No."}, {"t": "depends on $\\sigma$", "ok": false, "why": "Not on the diagonal."}]}]}, {"id": "valid", "title": "3.2 Valid kernels: how to decide in the exam", "html": "<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"bt\">Definition</div><p>A function $k(x,y)$ is a <b>valid kernel</b> if there exists some feature map $\\phi$ with $k(x,y)=\\phi(x)^T\\phi(y)$. Equivalent test: for <i>every</i> finite set of points, the Gram matrix $K_{ij}=k(x_i,x_j)$ is symmetric and positive semidefinite (PSD), i.e. $c^TKc\\ge0$ for every vector $c$, i.e. all eigenvalues $\\ge0$.</p></div><h3>Fast tests (use them in this order)</h3><ol><li><b>Symmetry:</b> $k(x,y)=k(y,x)$. Fails: not valid.</li><li><b>Diagonal:</b> $k(x,x)\\ge0$ for every $x$. Fails (even at one point such as $x=0$): not valid.</li><li><b>Cauchy-Schwarz:</b> $k(x,y)^2\\le k(x,x)\\,k(y,y)$. Fails: not valid.</li><li><b>Build it:</b> try to write it from known kernels with the rules below. Success: valid.</li><li><b>Two- or three-point Gram matrix:</b> compute its eigenvalues; one negative proves it invalid.</li></ol><h3>Building rules (if $k_1,k_2$ are valid)</h3><table><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Result</th></tr><tr><td>$a\\,k_1+b\\,k_2$, $a,b\\ge0$</td><td>valid</td></tr><tr><td>$k_1\\cdot k_2$</td><td>valid</td></tr><tr><td>$k_1+c$ with $c\\ge0$</td><td>valid</td></tr><tr><td>$f(x)\\,k_1(x,y)\\,f(y)$ for any function $f$</td><td>valid</td></tr><tr><td>$k_1^m$ for a positive integer $m$; $e^{k_1}$</td><td>valid</td></tr><tr><td>$k_1-k_2$, or a negative multiplier</td><td><b>not guaranteed</b>: check the tests</td></tr></table><p><b>Standard valid kernels:</b> $x^Ty$, $(x^Ty+c)^m$ with $c\\ge0$, Gaussian, Laplacian $\\exp(-\\|x-y\\|/\\sigma)$. <b>Standard invalid:</b> $\\|x-y\\|$, $\\|x-y\\|^2$, $(x^Ty-1)^3$ (diagonal at $0$ is $-1$).</p><div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example 9: $2k_1+5k_2$</div><p>$c^T(2K_1+5K_2)c=2c^TK_1c+5c^TK_2c\\ge0$, since both terms are $\\ge0$ and the weights are positive. Valid. This covers any non-negative combination, whether or not the weights add to $1$.</p></div><div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example 10: the distance $\\|x-y\\|$</div><p>Take two points with distance $a>0$. Gram matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}0&a\\\\a&0\\end{bmatrix}$, eigenvalues $\\pm a$. One is negative, so it is not PSD. Invalid.</p></div><div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example 11 (tricky): $K_1-K_2$ with $K_1=x^Ty$, $K_2$ Gaussian</div><p>At $x=y=0$: $K_1(0,0)-K_2(0,0)=0-1=-1<0$, so the diagonal test fails for every $\\sigma$. Invalid. And $K_2/\\sqrt{K_2(x,x)K_2(y,y)}$ is just $K_2$ (the diagonal is $1$), while $\\exp(-\\tfrac{\\|x\\|^2+\\|y\\|^2}{2\\sigma^2})\\exp(\\tfrac{x^Ty}{\\sigma^2})=\\exp(-\\tfrac{\\|x-y\\|^2}{2\\sigma^2})=K_2$ because $-\\|x\\|^2-\\|y\\|^2+2x^Ty=-\\|x-y\\|^2$. So options built from these are valid sums.</p></div><div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example 12: normalising a kernel</div><p>$k_1(x,y)=\\dfrac{100\\,k(x,y)}{\\sqrt{k(x,x)k(y,y)}}=100\\Big(\\dfrac{\\phi(x)}{\\|\\phi(x)\\|}\\Big)^T\\Big(\\dfrac{\\phi(y)}{\\|\\phi(y)\\|}\\Big)$ since $k(x,x)=\\|\\phi(x)\\|^2$. Put the $100$ inside as $\\sqrt{100}=10$ on each side: $\\phi_1(x)=10\\,\\phi(x)/\\|\\phi(x)\\|$. For $k=x^Ty$ this normalised kernel is the cosine similarity: $x=(3,4)$, $y=(4,3)$ give $24/25=0.96$.</p></div><div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"bt\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Saying \"subtraction is never valid\": $3k-k$ is valid. Check, do not assume.</li><li>Saying \"a kernel is never negative\": $x^Ty$ takes negative values. Only $k(x,x)\\ge0$ is required.</li><li>Rejecting the Laplacian because it is not squared: it is valid.</li><li>Putting $100$ instead of $\\sqrt{100}$ in a feature map.</li></ul></div><div class=\"explore\"><div class=\"bt\">Interactive explorer</div><p>Set the entries of a $2\\times2$ Gram matrix. The explorer checks PSD and, when possible, draws two arrows whose dot products produce the table.</p><div class=\"q1 q1w\"><div data-widget=\"gram2\" data-a=\"2\" data-b=\"1\" data-c=\"2\"></div></div></div>", "quiz": [{"id": "kq7", "concept": "valid", "level": "medium", "type": "mcq", "stem": "Is $(x^Ty)^2-1$ a valid kernel?", "options": [{"t": "No: at $x=0$, $k(0,0)=-1<0$", "ok": true, "why": "Correct."}, {"t": "Yes: it is a polynomial kernel", "ok": false, "why": "A negative constant breaks it."}, {"t": "Yes: squares are non-negative", "ok": false, "why": "The $-1$ makes the diagonal negative."}, {"t": "Only for $d=1$", "ok": false, "why": "Fails in every dimension."}]}, {"id": "kq8", "concept": "valid", "level": "easy", "type": "mcq", "stem": "If $k_1,k_2$ are valid, then $k_1k_2$ is", "options": [{"t": "valid", "ok": true, "why": "Correct: the product rule."}, {"t": "invalid", "ok": false, "why": "No."}, {"t": "valid only if both are Gaussian", "ok": false, "why": "Holds for any valid kernels."}, {"t": "valid only if $k_1=k_2$", "ok": false, "why": "Not needed."}]}]}, {"id": "kpca", "title": "3.4 Kernel PCA", "html": "<h3>Idea</h3><p>Dual PCA never needs the points themselves, only the table of dot products $x_i^Tx_j$. Replace each entry by $k(x_i,x_j)$ and the same algorithm runs in the feature space of $\\phi$: that is kernel PCA. It can find curved structure (for example two concentric rings) that ordinary PCA cannot.</p><div class=\"box formula\"><div class=\"bt\">Recipe</div><ol><li>Gram matrix $K_{ij}=k(x_i,x_j)$, size $n\\times n$.</li><li><b>Centre</b> it: $K^c=HKH$ with $H=I-\\tfrac1n\\mathbf 1\\mathbf 1^T$. Entry-wise: $K^c_{ij}=K_{ij}-\\theta_i-\\theta_j+P$ where $\\theta_i=\\tfrac1n\\sum_kK_{ik}$ (row mean) and $P=\\tfrac1{n^2}\\sum_{ij}K_{ij}$ (grand mean).</li><li>Eigen-decompose $K^c\\beta=\\lambda\\beta$ (largest $\\lambda$ first).</li><li>Variance of the PC: $\\lambda/n$. Explained variance ratio: $\\lambda/\\operatorname{tr}K^c$ (the $n$ cancels).</li><li>Training scores: $\\sqrt\\lambda\\,\\beta_i$ for point $i$ (with $\\|\\beta\\|=1$).</li></ol></div><p><b>How many components?</b> At most $\\min(D_{\\text{eff}},\\,n-1)$ non-zero ones, where $D_{\\text{eff}}$ is the rank of the feature space. The $n-1$ comes from centring (the centred points sum to zero). So kernel PCA can give <i>more than $d$</i> components (a linear kernel gives at most $\\min(d,n-1)$).</p><div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example 13 (exam): the centred Gram matrix</div><p>Points $x_1=(1,0,0)^T$, $x_2=(0,1,0)^T$ (columns of $X$), $k=(1+x_i^Tx_j)^2$. $K=\\begin{bmatrix}4&1\\\\1&4\\end{bmatrix}$. Row means $\\theta=(2.5,2.5)$, grand mean $P=2.5$. $K^c_{11}=4-2.5-2.5+2.5=1.5$, $K^c_{12}=1-2.5-2.5+2.5=-1.5$. So $K^c=\\begin{bmatrix}1.5&-1.5\\\\-1.5&1.5\\end{bmatrix}$: eigenvalues $3$ and $0$, so one component with variance $3/2=1.5$ and EVR $100\\%$.</p><p>Quick checks any centred Gram matrix must pass: symmetric, row sums $0$, diagonal $\\ge0$, PSD.</p></div><div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example 14: a $3\\times3$ entry</div><p>$K=\\begin{bmatrix}2&1&0\\\\1&2&1\\\\0&1&2\\end{bmatrix}$. $\\theta=(1,\\tfrac43,1)$, $P=\\tfrac{10}9$. $K^c_{12}=1-1-\\tfrac43+\\tfrac{10}9=-\\tfrac29\\approx-0.222$ (code: full $HKH$ gives the same).</p></div><div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example 15 (tricky): eigenvalue to variance</div><p>$n=100$ points, largest centred-Gram eigenvalue $10$. Covariance of the transformed data has largest eigenvalue $10/100=0.1$ (not $10$, and not $10/99$ with the course convention $\\tfrac1n$).</p></div><div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"bt\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Using $K$ instead of $K^c$, or forgetting $+P$ when centring.</li><li>Quoting $\\lambda$ as the variance (divide by $n$).</li><li>Assuming kernel PCA gives at most $d$ components.</li><li>Believing kernel PCA needs linearly separable data (it is unsupervised).</li></ul></div>", "quiz": [{"id": "kq9", "concept": "kpca", "level": "medium", "type": "num", "stem": "$K=\\begin{bmatrix}3&1\\\\1&3\\end{bmatrix}$. Centred entry $K^c_{11}$?", "num": {"ans": 1, "tol": 0.001, "show": "1"}, "explain": "$\\theta=(2,2)$, $P=2$: $3-2-2+2=1$.", "hint": ""}, {"id": "kq10", "concept": "kpca", "level": "easy", "type": "mcq", "stem": "Every row of a centred Gram matrix sums to", "options": [{"t": "$0$", "ok": true, "why": "Correct: centred features sum to zero."}, {"t": "$1$", "ok": false, "why": "No."}, {"t": "$n$", "ok": false, "why": "No."}, {"t": "the trace", "ok": false, "why": "No."}]}]}, {"id": "pyqs", "title": "All PYQs of this topic, solved", "kind": "pyq", "html": ""}, {"id": "revision", "title": "Revision page", "html": "<h3>Definitions to know cold</h3><table><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td>Feature map $\\phi$</td><td>$x\\mapsto\\phi(x)\\in\\mathbb R^D$, not unique</td></tr><tr><td>Kernel</td><td>$k(x,y)=\\phi(x)^T\\phi(y)$</td></tr><tr><td>Gram matrix</td><td>$K_{ij}=k(x_i,x_j)$, symmetric, PSD for a valid kernel</td></tr><tr><td>Centred Gram</td><td>$K^c=HKH$, row sums $0$</td></tr></table><h3>Formulas</h3><table><tr><th>Item</th><th>Formula</th></tr><tr><td>Polynomial features</td><td>$\\binom{d+m}{m}$ (with constant), $\\binom{d+m-1}{m}$ (homogeneous)</td></tr><tr><td>Gaussian</td><td>$\\exp(-\\|x-y\\|^2/2\\sigma^2)$, $k(x,x)=1$</td></tr><tr><td>Feature-space distance</td><td>$k(x,x)+k(y,y)-2k(x,y)$</td></tr><tr><td>Normalised kernel</td><td>$k/\\sqrt{k(x,x)k(y,y)}$</td></tr><tr><td>PC variance</td><td>$\\lambda_{K^c}/n$</td></tr><tr><td>Max components</td><td>$\\min(D_{\\text{eff}},n-1)$</td></tr></table><h3>Exam decision flow</h3><ol><li>\"Find $\\phi$\": expand, pair terms, $\\sqrt{\\text{coefficient}}$, keep the constant.</li><li>\"Value of kernel\": dot product first, then $+c$, then power; Gaussian: squared distance, divide by $2\\sigma^2$.</li><li>\"Valid?\": diagonal, symmetry, then building rules, then a tiny Gram matrix.</li><li>\"Kernel PCA\": centre, eigenvalues, divide by $n$, cap by $n-1$.</li></ol>"}, {"id": "test", "title": "Topic test: mixed questions", "kind": "test", "html": ""}, {"id": "checklist", "title": "Checklist: skills you should be able to do", "kind": "checklist", "html": ""}], "pyqs": [{"id": "kr01", "exam": "Jul 2023", "year": "2023", "qno": "Q2", "marks": 4, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "phi", "tag": "DERIVED", "sub": "3.1 Feature maps and kernels", "tagNote": "The source marks no correct option. The key (option 3) is derived by term matching [?].", "wording": "Consider the following kernel function $k:\\mathbb R^2\\times\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R$: $$k([x_1,x_2]^T,[y_1,y_2]^T)=1+x_1y_1+x_2y_2+x_1^2y_1^2x_2y_2+x_1y_1x_2^2y_2^2.$$ Find the appropriate transformation mapping $\\phi$ for the given kernel.", "options": ["$[1,\\ x_1x_2,\\ x_1^2x_2^2]^T$", "$[1,\\ x_1,\\ x_2,\\ x_1^2x_2^2]^T$", "$[1,\\ x_1,\\ x_2,\\ x_1^2x_2,\\ x_1x_2^2]^T$", "$[1,\\ x_1,\\ x_2,\\ x_1x_2,\\ x_1^2x_2^2]^T$"], "key": {"opts": [2]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the feature map $\\phi$ with $\\phi(x)^T\\phi(y)=k(x,y)$.</p>", "idea": "<p>Term matching: write each term of $k$ as (a function of $x$) times (the same function of $y$). Each such pair is one coordinate of $\\phi$.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>$1=(1)(1)$: coordinate $1$.</li><li>$x_1y_1$: coordinate $x_1$. $x_2y_2$: coordinate $x_2$.</li><li>$x_1^2y_1^2x_2y_2=(x_1^2x_2)(y_1^2y_2)$: coordinate $x_1^2x_2$.</li><li>$x_1y_1x_2^2y_2^2=(x_1x_2^2)(y_1y_2^2)$: coordinate $x_1x_2^2$.</li><li>So $\\phi=[1,x_1,x_2,x_1^2x_2,x_1x_2^2]^T$: option 3.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Split each term carefully: $x_1^2y_1^2x_2y_2$ has $x$-part $x_1^2x_2$ and $y$-part $y_1^2y_2$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Symbolic check: $\\phi(x)^T\\phi(y)-k=0$ identically for option 3; the other options differ by non-zero polynomials.</p>", "trap": "Pairing the factors wrongly, or being drawn to $x_1^2x_2^2$ because it looks tidy.", "rule": "<p>Kernel to feature map: split every term into an $x$-part times the same $y$-part.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Find $\\phi$ for $k(x,y)=1+x_1y_1+4x_2y_2+x_1x_2y_1y_2$ and compute $k((2,3),(1,-1))$.", "a": "$\\phi=[1,x_1,2x_2,x_1x_2]^T$ (since $4x_2y_2=(2x_2)(2y_2)$). $\\phi(2,3)=(1,2,6,6)$, $\\phi(1,-1)=(1,1,-2,-1)$, giving $1+2-12-6=-15$."}, "optWhy": ["Gives $x_1x_2y_1y_2$ and $x_1^2x_2^2y_1^2y_2^2$ and misses $x_1y_1$, $x_2y_2$.", "Gives $x_1^2x_2^2y_1^2y_2^2$ (degree 8) instead of the two mixed terms.", "<b>Correct.</b> All five terms match.", "Adds $x_1x_2y_1y_2$ and $x_1^2x_2^2y_1^2y_2^2$ that are not in $k$."]}}, {"id": "kr02", "exam": "Feb 2024", "year": "2024", "qno": "Q4", "marks": 4, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "phi", "tag": "DERIVED", "sub": "3.1 Feature maps and kernels", "tagNote": "No correct option is marked in this copy. The key (option 1) agrees with the Oct 2024 version [?].", "wording": "Consider the following kernel $k:\\mathbb R^2\\times\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R$, $k(x,y)=(x^Ty)^2+1$. Which of the following transformation mappings $\\phi$ may correspond to the kernel $k$?", "options": ["$[x_1^2,\\ \\sqrt2x_1x_2,\\ x_2^2,\\ 1]^T$", "$[x_1^2,\\ x_1+x_2,\\ x_2^2,\\ 1]^T$", "$[x_1,\\ \\sqrt2x_1^2x_2^2,\\ x_2,\\ 1]^T$", "$[x_1,\\ x_1x_2,\\ x_2,\\ 1]^T$"], "key": {"opts": [0]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> a feature map for $k(x,y)=(x^Ty)^2+1$.</p>", "idea": "<p>Expand $(x^Ty)^2$ and match terms. The cross term appears twice, so it needs the coefficient $\\sqrt2$ in $\\phi$.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>$(x^Ty)^2=(x_1y_1+x_2y_2)^2=x_1^2y_1^2+2x_1x_2y_1y_2+x_2^2y_2^2$.</li><li>Terms: $x_1^2$, $\\sqrt2\\,x_1x_2$ (since $2x_1x_2y_1y_2=(\\sqrt2x_1x_2)(\\sqrt2y_1y_2)$), $x_2^2$, and the constant $1$.</li><li>So $\\phi=[x_1^2,\\sqrt2x_1x_2,x_2^2,1]^T$: option 1.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>The same question reappears in Oct 2024 with option 1 marked correct, which agrees with this derivation.</p>", "verify": "<p>Symbolic: $\\phi(x)^T\\phi(y)-k=0$ for option 1 only. Test pair $(1,1),(1,-1)$: $k=1$; the options give $1,3,3,0$.</p>", "trap": "Forgetting the $\\sqrt2$ on the cross term. \"May correspond\" is used because rotated or sign-changed versions of $\\phi$ would also work, but they are not among the options.", "rule": "<p>A degree-$m$ polynomial kernel has coordinates of degree $m$ only (plus a constant if a constant is added).</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Give $\\phi$ for $k(x,y)=(x^Ty)^2+4$ and compute $k((2,3),(1,-1))$.", "a": "$\\phi=[x_1^2,\\sqrt2x_1x_2,x_2^2,2]^T$ (constant $4=2\\cdot2$). $x^Ty=-1$, so $k=1+4=5$."}, "optWhy": ["<b>Correct.</b>", "The coordinate $x_1+x_2$ squared produces mixed terms $x_1y_2$ and $x_2y_1$ that $k$ does not have.", "Has degree-1 coordinates $x_1,x_2$ and a degree-4 coordinate, none of which appear in $k$.", "Degree-1 coordinates add $x_1y_1+x_2y_2$, and $x_1^2,x_2^2$ are missing; the cross coefficient should be $\\sqrt2$."]}}, {"id": "kr03", "exam": "Oct 2024 (27 Oct)", "year": "2024", "qno": "Q3", "marks": 4, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "phi", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "3.1 Feature maps and kernels", "wording": "Consider the kernel $k:\\mathbb R^2\\times\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R$, $k(x,y)=(x^Ty)^2+1$. Which transformation mapping $\\phi$ may correspond to this kernel?", "options": ["$[x_1^2,\\ \\sqrt2x_1x_2,\\ x_2^2,\\ 1]^T$", "$[x_1^2,\\ x_1+x_2,\\ x_2^2,\\ 1]^T$", "$[x_1,\\ \\sqrt2x_1^2x_2^2,\\ x_2,\\ 1]^T$", "$[x_1,\\ x_1x_2,\\ x_2,\\ 1]^T$"], "key": {"opts": [0]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> a feature map for $k(x,y)=(x^Ty)^2+1$.</p>", "idea": "<p>Expand $(x^Ty)^2$ and match terms. The cross term appears twice, so it needs the coefficient $\\sqrt2$ in $\\phi$.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>$(x^Ty)^2=(x_1y_1+x_2y_2)^2=x_1^2y_1^2+2x_1x_2y_1y_2+x_2^2y_2^2$.</li><li>Terms: $x_1^2$, $\\sqrt2\\,x_1x_2$ (since $2x_1x_2y_1y_2=(\\sqrt2x_1x_2)(\\sqrt2y_1y_2)$), $x_2^2$, and the constant $1$.</li><li>So $\\phi=[x_1^2,\\sqrt2x_1x_2,x_2^2,1]^T$: option 1.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Identical question to Feb 2024 Q4; option order can change between papers, so identify the option by its terms, not its position. A rotated $\\phi'=Q\\phi$ would also be valid, but only the standard form is listed.</p>", "verify": "<p>Symbolic: $\\phi(x)^T\\phi(y)-k=0$ for option 1 only. Test pair $(1,1),(1,-1)$: $k=1$; the options give $1,3,3,0$.</p>", "trap": "Forgetting the $\\sqrt2$ on the cross term. \"May correspond\" is used because rotated or sign-changed versions of $\\phi$ would also work, but they are not among the options.", "rule": "<p>A degree-$m$ polynomial kernel has coordinates of degree $m$ only (plus a constant if a constant is added).</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Give $\\phi$ for $k(x,y)=(x^Ty)^2+4$ and compute $k((2,3),(1,-1))$.", "a": "$\\phi=[x_1^2,\\sqrt2x_1x_2,x_2^2,2]^T$ (constant $4=2\\cdot2$). $x^Ty=-1$, so $k=1+4=5$."}, "optWhy": ["<b>Correct.</b>", "The coordinate $x_1+x_2$ squared produces mixed terms $x_1y_2$ and $x_2y_1$ that $k$ does not have.", "Has degree-1 coordinates $x_1,x_2$ and a degree-4 coordinate, none of which appear in $k$.", "Degree-1 coordinates add $x_1y_1+x_2y_2$, and $x_1^2,x_2^2$ are missing; the cross coefficient should be $\\sqrt2$."]}}, {"id": "kr04", "exam": "Mar 2026 (15 Mar)", "year": "2026", "qno": "Q4", "marks": 3, "type": "SA", "concept": "poly", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "3.3 Polynomial and Gaussian kernels", "tagNote": "The kernel form is not printed; the key $35$ matches $(1+x^Ty)^3$ [?].", "wording": "Consider a polynomial kernel of degree 3 on a feature space of dimension 4. How many features will the transformed space have?", "key": {"lo": 35, "hi": 35, "show": "35"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the number of coordinates of $\\phi$ for the polynomial kernel $(1+x^Ty)^3$ on $\\mathbb R^4$.</p>", "idea": "<p>The kernel $(1+x^Ty)^m$ includes every monomial of degree $0$ up to $m$. The count is $\\binom{d+m}{m}$.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Here $d=4$, $m=3$: $\\binom{4+3}{3}=\\binom73=35$.</li><li>By degree: degree $0$: $1$; degree $1$: $4$; degree $2$: $\\binom{5}{2}=10$; degree $3$: $\\binom63=20$. Total $1+4+10+20=35$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>The coordinates are monomials scaled by $\\sqrt{\\text{multinomial coefficient}}$ so that $\\phi(x)^T\\phi(y)=(1+x^Ty)^3$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code enumerated exponent tuples: $35$ monomials of degree $\\le3$ in $4$ variables ($20$ of degree exactly $3$).</p>", "trap": "$\\binom{d+m-1}{m}=20$ counts only the homogeneous kernel $(x^Ty)^3$; or $34$ if the constant is forgotten.", "rule": "<p>$(1+x^Ty)^m$ on $\\mathbb R^d$ has $\\binom{d+m}{m}$ features; $(x^Ty)^m$ has $\\binom{d+m-1}{m}$.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "$(1+x^Ty)^2$ with input dimension $5$: how many features? And for $(x^Ty)^2$?", "a": "$\\binom72=21$; homogeneous: $\\binom62=15$."}}}, {"id": "kr05", "exam": "Jul 2026 (16 Jul)", "year": "2026", "qno": "Q6", "marks": 3, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "phi", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "3.1 Feature maps and kernels", "wording": "Consider the kernel function for scalar inputs $K(x_1,x_2)=10x_1^2x_2^2$. Find a feature map $\\phi:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ such that $K(x_1,x_2)=\\phi(x_1)^T\\phi(x_2)$.", "options": ["$\\phi(x)=10x^2$", "$\\phi(x)=x^2$", "$\\phi(x)=\\sqrt{10}\\,x$", "$\\phi(x)=\\sqrt{10}\\,x^2$"], "key": {"opts": [3]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> a scalar feature map for $10x_1^2x_2^2$.</p>", "idea": "<p>For scalar inputs $K(x_1,x_2)=\\phi(x_1)\\phi(x_2)$, so split the term into an $x_1$-part and an $x_2$-part, each carrying $\\sqrt{10}$.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>$10x_1^2x_2^2=(\\sqrt{10}x_1^2)(\\sqrt{10}x_2^2)$, so $\\phi(x)=\\sqrt{10}\\,x^2$.</li><li>Check at $x_1=2,x_2=3$: $K=10\\cdot4\\cdot9=360$; option 4 gives $\\sqrt{10}\\cdot4\\cdot\\sqrt{10}\\cdot9=360$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>$-\\sqrt{10}x^2$ would also work (sign is free) but it is not listed.</p>", "verify": "<p>Products at $(2,3)$: $3600,36,60,360$ versus $K=360$.</p>", "trap": "Forgetting that the constant is split between the two factors, so each side gets $\\sqrt{10}$.", "rule": "<p>Constant $c$ in a kernel: put $\\sqrt c$ in the feature map.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Scalars: find $\\phi$ for $K=7x_1x_2$ and compute $K(2,3)$; find $\\phi$ for $K=\\tfrac14x_1^2x_2^2$.", "a": "$\\phi=\\sqrt7x$, $K(2,3)=42$; $\\phi=\\tfrac12x^2$ (and $K(2,3)=9$)."}, "optWhy": ["Gives $100x_1^2x_2^2$.", "Gives $x_1^2x_2^2$, missing the factor $10$.", "Gives $10x_1x_2$, wrong degree.", "<b>Correct.</b>"]}}, {"id": "kr06", "exam": "Jul 2024 (07 Jul)", "year": "2024", "qno": "Q7", "marks": 4, "type": "SA", "concept": "poly", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "3.3 Polynomial and Gaussian kernels", "tagNote": "The kernel form is not printed; the key $16$ matches $(1+x^Ty)^4$ [?].", "wording": "$k:\\mathbb R^2\\times\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R$ is a polynomial kernel of degree 4. Find $k\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\-1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2\\\\1\\end{bmatrix}\\right)$.", "key": {"lo": 16, "hi": 16, "show": "16"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the value of the degree-4 polynomial kernel $(1+x^Ty)^4$.</p>", "idea": "<p>The polynomial kernel of degree $m$ is $k(x,y)=(1+x^Ty)^m$ (the $+1$ includes all lower-degree terms).</p>", "method": "<ol><li>$x^Ty=1\\cdot2+(-1)\\cdot1=1$.</li><li>$k=(1+1)^4=2^4=16$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Check through the expansion: $(1+x_1y_1+x_2y_2)^4$ with $x_1y_1=2$, $x_2y_2=-1$ gives $(1+2-1)^4=16$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: an explicit 15-coordinate $\\phi$ with multinomial weights gives $\\phi(x)^T\\phi(y)=16$.</p>", "trap": "A sign slip in $x^Ty$ ($2+1=3$ would give $4^4=256$), or forgetting the $+1$ (giving $1$).", "rule": "<p>Polynomial kernel: dot product first, add $1$, then raise to the power.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Degree 3, $x=(2,1)$, $y=(1,1)$: $k$ with $c=1$? with $c=2$?", "a": "$x^Ty=3$; $4^3=64$; $5^3=125$."}}}, {"id": "kr07", "exam": "Mar 2026 (15 Mar)", "year": "2026", "qno": "Q5", "marks": 3, "type": "SA", "concept": "gauss", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "3.3 Polynomial and Gaussian kernels", "tagNote": "The kernel form is not printed; the key matches $e^{-\\|x-y\\|^2/2\\sigma^2}$ [?].", "wording": "Consider the Gaussian kernel $k:\\mathbb R^2\\times\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R$ with $\\sigma=1$. Compute $k((0,1),(1,0))$. Enter the answer correct to two decimal places.", "key": {"lo": 0.34, "hi": 0.4, "show": "0.34 to 0.40"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> $k(x,y)=e^{-\\|x-y\\|^2/(2\\sigma^2)}$ for the two points.</p>", "idea": "<p>The Gaussian kernel is a similarity: $1$ for identical points, close to $0$ for far points.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>$\\|x-y\\|^2=(0-1)^2+(1-0)^2=2$.</li><li>$2\\sigma^2=2$.</li><li>$k=e^{-2/2}=e^{-1}=0.3679\\approx0.37$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Sanity: $0<k\\le1$, and $k<1$ because $x\\ne y$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: $e^{-1}=0.367879$.</p>", "trap": "Using $\\|x-y\\|$ without squaring ($0.49$), or $\\sigma^2$ instead of $2\\sigma^2$ ($0.135$).", "rule": "<p>Gaussian: $\\exp(-\\|x-y\\|^2/2\\sigma^2)$.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "$\\sigma=2$, $x=(0,0)$, $y=(1,3)$. $k$ to two decimals?", "a": "$\\|x-y\\|^2=10$, $2\\sigma^2=8$, $e^{-1.25}=0.29$."}}}, {"id": "kr08", "exam": "Jul 2023", "year": "2023", "qno": "Q8", "marks": 3.5, "type": "MSQ", "concept": "valid", "tag": "DERIVED", "sub": "3.2 Valid kernels", "tagNote": "The source marks no correct option. The key (1 and 3) is derived [?].", "wording": "Which of the following statements is/are true?", "options": ["$k(x_1,x_2)=(x_1^Tx_2)^3$ on $\\mathbb R^2$ is a valid kernel.", "$k(x_1,x_2)=-(x_1^Tx_2)^3$ on $\\mathbb R^2$ is a valid kernel.", "Nonzero eigenvalues of $XX^T$ and $X^TX$ are the same.", "Eigenvectors of $XX^T$ are same as eigenvectors of $X^TX$."], "key": {"opts": [0, 2]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> which statements about kernels and Gram matrices are true.</p>", "idea": "<p>A kernel is valid if it equals $\\phi(x)^T\\phi(y)$ (equivalently, its Gram matrix is symmetric PSD). A valid kernel has $k(x,x)\\ge0$. Also $XX^T$ and $X^TX$ share non-zero eigenvalues.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Option 1 ($(x^Ty)^3$): expand in $\\mathbb R^2$; it equals $\\phi(x)^T\\phi(y)$ with $\\phi=[x_1^3,\\sqrt3x_1^2x_2,\\sqrt3x_1x_2^2,x_2^3]$. Valid.</li><li>Option 2 ($-(x^Ty)^3$): at $x=(1,0)$, $k(x,x)=-1<0$. A valid kernel cannot be negative on the diagonal. Invalid.</li><li>Option 3: non-zero eigenvalues of $XX^T$ and $X^TX$ coincide. True.</li><li>Option 4: eigenvectors live in different spaces; they are related by $X$, not equal. False.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Marks are printed as $3.5$ in the source. The same statements appear in Feb 2025 Q6, where the key confirms options 1 and 3 (and 4 false).</p>", "verify": "<p>200 random 6-point sets in $\\mathbb R^2$: the smallest eigenvalue of the $(x^Ty)^3$ Gram matrix is $\\ge-10^{-9}$; $k(x,x)=-1$ for option 2.</p>", "trap": "Assuming the negative of a valid kernel is valid, or equating same eigenvalues with same eigenvectors.", "rule": "<p>Positive multiples and powers of valid kernels stay valid; negative multiples break $k(x,x)\\ge0$.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "On $\\mathbb R^2$ decide: (i) $5(x^Ty)^3+2x^Ty$ (ii) $(x^Ty)^2-1$.", "a": "(i) valid: non-negative combination of valid kernels. (ii) invalid: $k(0,0)=-1<0$."}, "optWhy": ["<b>True.</b>", "False: negative diagonal.", "<b>True.</b>", "False: only the eigenvalues agree."]}}, {"id": "kr09", "exam": "Feb 2024", "year": "2024", "qno": "Q3", "marks": 4, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "valid", "tag": "DERIVED", "sub": "3.2 Valid kernels", "tagNote": "The source marks no correct option. The key (No) is derived, reading \"valid kernel\" as \"for every valid $k$\" [?].", "wording": "Let $k:\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ be a valid kernel. Is $\\sin(x_1)\\cos(x_2)\\,k(x_1,x_2)$ a valid kernel? Here, $x_1,x_2\\in\\mathbb R$.", "options": ["Yes", "No"], "key": {"opts": [1]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> whether multiplying a valid kernel by $\\sin(x_1)\\cos(x_2)$ always gives a valid kernel.</p>", "idea": "<p>The closure rule is $f(x)\\,k(x,y)\\,f(y)$ with the <i>same</i> function on both sides. Different functions $\\sin$ and $\\cos$ break symmetry.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Take $k\\equiv1$ (valid). Then $k_\\star(x_1,x_2)=\\sin(x_1)\\cos(x_2)$.</li><li>$k_\\star(\\pi/2,0)=1$ but $k_\\star(0,\\pi/2)=0$: not symmetric, so not a kernel.</li><li>Also $k_\\star(3\\pi/4,3\\pi/4)=-\\tfrac12<0$ on the diagonal.</li><li>One valid $k$ that fails is enough to refute \"always valid\": answer No.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>It would be valid with $\\sin(x_1)\\sin(x_2)$ (same $f$ on both sides).</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: $k_\\star(\\pi/2,0)=1.0$, $k_\\star(0,\\pi/2)=0.0$.</p>", "trap": "Treating $\\sin\\cdot\\cos$ as just \"a function\" and applying the product rule, which needs both factors to be valid kernels.", "rule": "<p>$f(x)k(x,y)f(y)$ is valid; $g(x)k(x,y)h(y)$ with $g\\ne h$ is not guaranteed.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "For valid $k$: (i) is $\\cos(x_1)\\cos(x_2)(1+x_1x_2)$ valid? (ii) is $x_1^2k(x_1,x_2)x_2$ always valid?", "a": "(i) yes ($f=\\cos$ on both sides, $1+x_1x_2$ valid). (ii) no: with $k\\equiv1$, $k_\\star(1,2)=2\\ne k_\\star(2,1)=4$."}, "optWhy": ["False: a counterexample exists.", "<b>Correct.</b>"]}}, {"id": "kr10", "exam": "Jul 2024 (07 Jul)", "year": "2024", "qno": "Q3", "marks": 3, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "valid", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "3.2 Valid kernels", "wording": "Let $x,y\\in\\mathbb R^2$. Define $k_1(x,y)=x_1y_1+x_2y_2+x_1x_2y_1y_2$ and $k_2(x,y)=x_1y_1+x_2y_2+x_1x_2y_1y_2+1$. Which statement is true?", "options": ["Both $k_1$ and $k_2$ are valid kernels.", "$k_1$ is valid, but $k_2$ is not.", "$k_2$ is valid, but $k_1$ is not.", "Neither is valid."], "key": {"opts": [0]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the validity of $k_1$ and $k_2=k_1+1$.</p>", "idea": "<p>A sum of terms of the form $f(x)f(y)$ with non-negative coefficients is a valid kernel (each term is a rank-one PSD Gram matrix).</p>", "method": "<ol><li>$k_1=\\phi_1(x)^T\\phi_1(y)$ with $\\phi_1=[x_1,x_2,x_1x_2]^T$: valid.</li><li>$k_2=k_1+1$ with the constant $1=1\\cdot1$: $\\phi_2=[x_1,x_2,x_1x_2,1]^T$: valid.</li><li>Gram test: $c^T(K+\\mathbf 1\\mathbf 1^T)c=c^TKc+(\\sum c_i)^2\\ge0$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>$x_1x_2y_1y_2$ is $f(x)f(y)$ with $f=x_1x_2$, so it is fine.</p>", "verify": "<p>200 random 6-point sets: smallest eigenvalues of both Gram matrices $\\ge-10^{-9}$.</p>", "trap": "Thinking \"$+1$ breaks validity\" or that a cross-looking term is invalid.", "rule": "<p>Non-negative sums of $f(x)f(y)$ terms (including constants) are valid.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Decide: (a) $4x_1y_1+x_1x_2y_1y_2+2$ (b) $x_1y_1-x_2y_2$.", "a": "(a) valid with $\\phi=(2x_1,x_1x_2,\\sqrt2)$. (b) invalid: $k((0,1),(0,1))=-1<0$."}, "optWhy": ["<b>Correct.</b>", "Adding a constant keeps validity.", "$k_1$ is valid (explicit $\\phi_1$).", "Both are valid."]}}, {"id": "kr11", "exam": "Oct 2024 (27 Oct)", "year": "2024", "qno": "Q2", "marks": 4, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "valid", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "3.2 Valid kernels", "wording": "Let $x,y\\in\\mathbb R^2$. Define $k_1(x,y)=x_1y_1+x_2y_2+(x_1+x_2)(y_1+y_2)$ and $k_2(x,y)=x_1y_1+x_2y_2+(x_1^2+y_2^2)+3$. Which statement is true?", "options": ["Both $k_1$ and $k_2$ are valid kernels.", "$k_1$ is a valid kernel, but $k_2$ is not a valid kernel.", "$k_2$ is a valid kernel, but $k_1$ is not a valid kernel.", "Neither $k_1$ nor $k_2$ is a valid kernel."], "key": {"opts": [1]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> which of $k_1,k_2$ is a valid kernel.</p>", "idea": "<p>A valid kernel must be symmetric. A sum is valid only when every piece is a valid kernel; $x_1^2+y_2^2$ is an additive mix of $x$ and $y$, not a product of an $x$-part and a $y$-part.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>$k_1=x_1y_1+x_2y_2+(x_1+x_2)(y_1+y_2)$ equals $\\phi(x)^T\\phi(y)$ with $\\phi=[x_1,x_2,x_1+x_2]^T$. Valid.</li><li>$k_2$: test symmetry. $k_2((1,0),(0,0))=0+(1+0)+3=4$ but $k_2((0,0),(1,0))=0+(0+0)+3=3$. Not symmetric, so not valid.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>The sum of $x_1^2$ and $y_2^2$ is not a dot product (dot products multiply).</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: $k_2((1,0),(0,0))=4$, $k_2((0,0),(1,0))=3$; Grams of $k_1$ on 200 random sets are PSD.</p>", "trap": "\"A sum is valid\" without checking that each term is a kernel.", "rule": "<p>Kernel tests: symmetry first, then $k(x,x)\\ge0$, then explicit $\\phi$ or closure.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Is $k(x,y)=(x_1+y_1)^2$ on $\\mathbb R^2$ valid? Give a test.", "a": "No. For $x_1=1$ and $x_1=2$: $K=\\begin{bmatrix}4&9\\\\9&16\\end{bmatrix}$, $\\det=-17<0$; also $9^2>4\\cdot16$ breaks Cauchy–Schwarz."}, "optWhy": ["$k_2$ fails symmetry.", "<b>Correct.</b>", "Reversed.", "$k_1$ is valid."]}}, {"id": "kr12", "exam": "Feb 2025 (23 Feb)", "year": "2025", "qno": "Q5", "marks": 4, "type": "MSQ", "concept": "valid", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "3.2 Valid kernels", "wording": "Which of the following matrices are appropriate as $K=X^TX$ for some data matrix $X$?", "options": ["$\\begin{bmatrix}4&1&2\\\\1&5&3\\\\2&3&6\\end{bmatrix}$", "$\\begin{bmatrix}-2&-1&0\\\\-1&-3&-1\\\\0&-1&-1\\end{bmatrix}$", "$\\begin{bmatrix}1&-2&1\\\\2&5&-2\\\\1&-2&2\\end{bmatrix}$", "$\\begin{bmatrix}3&1&0\\\\1&4&1\\\\0&1&5\\end{bmatrix}$"], "key": {"opts": [0, 3]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> which matrices can be Gram matrices $X^TX$.</p>", "idea": "<p>$K=X^TX$ exactly when $K$ is symmetric and positive semi-definite (all eigenvalues $\\ge0$).</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Option 1 $\\begin{bmatrix}4&1&2\\\\1&5&3\\\\2&3&6\\end{bmatrix}$: symmetric; leading minors $4,19,\\ldots>0$ (all eigenvalues positive). Valid.</li><li>Option 2: the diagonal is negative ($x^TKx<0$ for $e_1$): not PSD.</li><li>Option 3: $K_{12}=-2\\ne K_{21}=2$: not symmetric.</li><li>Option 4 $\\begin{bmatrix}3&1&0\\\\1&4&1\\\\0&1&5\\end{bmatrix}$: symmetric, diagonally dominant with positive diagonal, so PSD. Valid.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Negative off-diagonal entries are fine ($\\begin{bmatrix}2&-1\\\\-1&2\\end{bmatrix}$ is PSD); positive diagonal plus symmetry is not enough ($\\begin{bmatrix}1&2\\\\2&1\\end{bmatrix}$ has eigenvalue $-1$).</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: options 1 and 4 have all eigenvalues positive and Cholesky factors give $X^TX=K$; option 2 eigenvalues $-3.88,-1.65,-0.47$.</p>", "trap": "Rejecting a matrix just because of negative off-diagonal entries.", "rule": "<p>Gram matrix $\\iff$ symmetric and PSD.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Can $\\begin{bmatrix}2&3\\\\3&4\\end{bmatrix}$ or $\\begin{bmatrix}4&2\\\\2&1\\end{bmatrix}$ be $X^TX$? If so give $X$.", "a": "First: $\\det=-1<0$, no. Second: eigenvalues $0,5$, yes; $X=[2\\ \\ 1]$ gives $X^TX=\\begin{bmatrix}4&2\\\\2&1\\end{bmatrix}$."}, "optWhy": ["<b>Valid.</b>", "Negative diagonal.", "Not symmetric.", "<b>Valid.</b>"]}}, {"id": "kr13", "exam": "Feb 2025 (23 Feb)", "year": "2025", "qno": "Q6", "marks": 3, "type": "MSQ", "concept": "valid", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "3.2 Valid kernels", "wording": "Which of the following statements is/are true?", "options": ["$k(x_1,x_2)=(x_1^Tx_2)^3$ on $\\mathbb R^2$ is a valid kernel.", "$k(x_1,x_2)=e^{-\\|x_1-x_2\\|^2}$ on $\\mathbb R^n$ is a valid kernel.", "The trace of $XX^T$ is equal to the trace of $X^TX$.", "Eigenvectors of $XX^T$ are the same as eigenvectors of $X^TX$."], "key": {"opts": [0, 1, 2]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> which statements about kernels and Gram matrices are true.</p>", "idea": "<p>Polynomial and Gaussian kernels are valid; $\\operatorname{tr}(XX^T)=\\operatorname{tr}(X^TX)=\\sum_{ij}X_{ij}^2$; eigenvectors are not shared.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Option 1: $(x^Ty)^3$ on $\\mathbb R^2$ is valid (explicit $\\phi$ with $\\sqrt3$ weights).</li><li>Option 2: $e^{-\\|x_1-x_2\\|^2}$ is the Gaussian kernel with $\\sigma^2=\\tfrac12$: valid for any $n$.</li><li>Option 3: both traces equal the sum of squares of all entries of $X$. True.</li><li>Option 4: eigenvectors live in $\\mathbb R^d$ and $\\mathbb R^n$; only non-zero eigenvalues match. False.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>The Gaussian kernel is valid for every $\\sigma>0$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: random $4\\times7$ gives trace $21.1572$ for both; $X=\\begin{bmatrix}1&1\\\\0&1\\end{bmatrix}$ gives $3$ and $3$.</p>", "trap": "Restricting the Gaussian to $\\sigma=1$, or thinking different sizes force different traces.", "rule": "<p>Valid: polynomial, Gaussian, non-negative combinations. Traces and non-zero eigenvalues agree; eigenvectors do not.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Decide: (i) $e^{-3\\|x-y\\|^2}$ (ii) $e^{+\\|x-y\\|^2}$ (iii) $\\operatorname{tr}((XX^T)^2)=\\operatorname{tr}((X^TX)^2)$.", "a": "(i) valid ($\\sigma^2=\\tfrac16$). (ii) invalid: with $x=0,y=1$, $k(x,y)^2=e^2>k(x,x)k(y,y)=1$. (iii) true (same non-zero eigenvalues)."}, "optWhy": ["<b>True.</b>", "<b>True.</b>", "<b>True.</b>", "False."]}}, {"id": "kr14", "exam": "Jul 2025 (13 Jul)", "year": "2025", "qno": "Q4", "marks": 4, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "valid", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "3.2 Valid kernels", "wording": "Let $k:\\mathbb R^d\\times\\mathbb R^d\\to\\mathbb R$ be a valid kernel function such that $k(x,y)=\\phi(x)^T\\phi(y)$, where $\\phi:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\mathbb R^D$, $d<D$. Define $k_1(x,y)=\\dfrac{100\\,k(x,y)}{\\sqrt{k(x,x)\\,k(y,y)}}$. Find $\\phi_1:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\mathbb R^D$ such that $k_1(x,y)=\\phi_1(x)^T\\phi_1(y)$. Note: $\\|x\\|_2=\\sqrt{x^Tx}$.", "options": ["$\\phi_1(x)=\\dfrac{100\\,\\phi(x)}{\\|\\phi(x)\\|_2}$", "$\\phi_1(x)=\\dfrac{\\phi(x)}{10\\,\\|\\phi(x)\\|_2}$", "$\\phi_1(x)=\\dfrac{10\\,\\phi(x)}{\\|\\phi(x)\\|_2}$", "Such a $\\phi_1$ does not exist, i.e. $k_1$ is not a valid kernel."], "key": {"opts": [2]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> a feature map for the normalised, scaled kernel $k_1$.</p>", "idea": "<p>$k(x,x)=\\|\\phi(x)\\|^2$, so $\\sqrt{k(x,x)}=\\|\\phi(x)\\|$. Dividing each $\\phi$ by its length gives unit vectors; multiplying a kernel by $c$ multiplies each feature map by $\\sqrt c$.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>$k_1(x,y)=100\\,\\dfrac{\\phi(x)^T\\phi(y)}{\\|\\phi(x)\\|\\,\\|\\phi(y)\\|}=100\\,\\Big(\\dfrac{\\phi(x)}{\\|\\phi(x)\\|}\\Big)^T\\Big(\\dfrac{\\phi(y)}{\\|\\phi(y)\\|}\\Big)$.</li><li>Put the $100$ inside as $\\sqrt{100}=10$ on each side: $\\phi_1(x)=\\dfrac{10\\,\\phi(x)}{\\|\\phi(x)\\|}$. Option 3.</li><li>Check: $k_1(x,x)=\\|\\phi_1(x)\\|^2=100$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>With $\\phi(x)=x$ and test points: $k_1=50.8624$ and option 3 gives the same; option 1 gives $5086.24$, option 2 gives $0.005086$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code with random $5$-dim $\\phi$: option 3 matches $k_1$ exactly and $k_1(x,x)=100$.</p>", "trap": "Putting $100$ (not $\\sqrt{100}$) in the feature map, or thinking division destroys validity.", "rule": "<p>Scale a kernel by $c\\ge0$: scale $\\phi$ by $\\sqrt c$. Normalising: divide $\\phi$ by $\\|\\phi\\|$.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "For the same $k$ define $k_3=\\dfrac{9\\,k(x,y)}{\\sqrt{k(x,x)k(y,y)}}+16$. Give $\\phi_3$ and $k_3(x,x)$.", "a": "$\\phi_3(x)=(3\\phi(x)/\\|\\phi(x)\\|,\\ 4)$; $k_3(x,x)=9+16=25$."}, "optWhy": ["Gives $100^2=10^4$ times the cosine.", "Gives $\\tfrac1{100}$ times the cosine.", "<b>Correct.</b>", "Division by $\\sqrt{k(x,x)k(y,y)}$ is the same function sandwiched on both sides, so $k_1$ is valid."]}}, {"id": "kr15", "exam": "Oct 2025 (26 Oct)", "year": "2025", "qno": "Q6", "marks": 3, "type": "MSQ", "concept": "valid", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "3.2 Valid kernels", "tagNote": "The source wrote the norm as $|\\cdot|$; read as the Euclidean norm (the verdicts also hold for the $\\ell_1$ norm).", "wording": "Which of the following are valid kernel functions? Select all that apply.", "options": ["$K(x,y)=\\exp\\!\\left(-\\dfrac{\\|x-y\\|^2}{2\\sigma^2}\\right)$, $\\sigma>0$", "$K(x,y)=(x^Ty+c)^d$, $c\\ge0$, $d\\in\\mathbb N$", "$K(x,y)=\\|x-y\\|$", "$K(x,y)=\\exp\\!\\left(-\\dfrac{\\|x-y\\|}{\\sigma}\\right)$, $\\sigma>0$"], "key": {"opts": [0, 1, 3]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> which listed functions are valid kernels.</p>", "idea": "<p>Use the standard library: Gaussian, polynomial with $c\\ge0$ and Laplacian are valid; a plain distance is not (its diagonal is $0$ but off-diagonal entries are positive).</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Option 1 (Gaussian, $\\sigma>0$): valid.</li><li>Option 2 ($(x^Ty+c)^d$, $c\\ge0$, $d\\in\\mathbb N$): valid (power of a valid kernel plus a non-negative constant).</li><li>Option 3 ($\\|x-y\\|$): $K(x,x)=0$ but $K(x,y)>0$ for $x\\ne y$. For two points $x\\ne y$ the Gram matrix is $\\begin{bmatrix}0&a\\\\a&0\\end{bmatrix}$ with eigenvalues $\\pm a$, one negative. Invalid.</li><li>Option 4 (Laplacian $\\exp(-\\|x-y\\|/\\sigma)$): valid; it is a mixture of Gaussians over the scale parameter.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>$e^{-|x-y|^p}$ is valid for $p\\le2$ but not for $p=3$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: 2000 random 5-point Grams in $\\mathbb R^2$: Gaussian, $(x^Ty+1)^3$ and Laplacian never fail; $\\|x-y\\|$ fails every time.</p>", "trap": "Thinking distance is a similarity and so a kernel, or rejecting the Laplacian because it is not squared.", "rule": "<p>Valid: Gaussian, Laplacian, polynomial with $c\\ge0$. Invalid: plain distance, negative combinations.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Decide: (a) $e^{-\\|x-y\\|^2}+e^{-\\|x-y\\|}$ (b) $\\|x-y\\|^2$ (c) $(x^Ty-1)^3$.", "a": "(a) valid (sum of valid). (b) invalid: Gram $\\begin{bmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{bmatrix}$ has eigenvalue $-1$. (c) invalid: $K(0,0)=-1<0$."}, "optWhy": ["<b>Valid.</b>", "<b>Valid.</b>", "Invalid: Gram eigenvalues $\\pm a$.", "<b>Valid.</b> (Also valid with the $\\ell_1$ norm.)"]}}, {"id": "kr16", "exam": "Mar 2026 (15 Mar)", "year": "2026", "qno": "Q14", "marks": 2, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "valid", "tag": "DERIVED", "sub": "3.2 Valid kernels", "tagNote": "No correct option is marked in this copy; the key (True) is the standard linear-combination property [?].", "wording": "If $k_1:\\mathbb R^2\\times\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R$ and $k_2:\\mathbb R^2\\times\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R$ are valid kernels, then $2k_1+5k_2$ is also a valid kernel. (True/False)", "options": ["True", "False"], "key": {"opts": [0]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> whether a non-negative combination of valid kernels is valid.</p>", "idea": "<p>For any vector $c$, $c^T(2K_1+5K_2)c=2c^TK_1c+5c^TK_2c\\ge0$ because both Gram matrices are PSD and the weights are non-negative.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Gram matrix of $2k_1+5k_2$ is $2K_1+5K_2$, symmetric.</li><li>$c^T(2K_1+5K_2)c=2\\,c^TK_1c+5\\,c^TK_2c\\ge0$. So it is PSD: valid. True.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>The coefficients do not have to sum to $1$; any $a,b\\ge0$ work.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: for random $c$, $c^T(2K_1+5K_2)c=9.97305=2c^TK_1c+5c^TK_2c$; 2000 random Grams never fail.</p>", "trap": "Treating $2k_1-5k_2$ as valid, or requiring a convex combination.", "rule": "<p>Non-negative combinations, products and $f(x)kf(y)$ preserve validity; differences do not.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "$k_1,k_2$ valid. (i) is $k_1k_2$ valid? (ii) \"$3k_1-k_2$ is always valid\": true or false? (iii) is $k_1+k_2+7$ valid?", "a": "(i) yes (product rule). (ii) false: $k_1=x^Ty$, $k_2$ Gaussian, $x=0$ gives $-1<0$. (iii) yes (adding a non-negative constant)."}, "optWhy": ["<b>True.</b>", "False would need a counterexample, and none exists."]}}, {"id": "kr17", "exam": "Jul 2026 (16 Jul)", "year": "2026", "qno": "Q8", "marks": 4, "type": "MSQ", "concept": "valid", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "3.2 Valid kernels", "wording": "Assume that $K_1(x,y)=x^Ty$ and $K_2(x,y)=\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{\\|x-y\\|^2}{2\\sigma^2}\\right)$ are valid positive-semidefinite kernels. Which of the following are valid kernels?", "options": ["$\\dfrac{K_2(x,y)}{\\sqrt{K_2(x,x)}\\sqrt{K_2(y,y)}}+x^Ty$", "$x^Ty+\\exp\\!\\left(-\\dfrac{\\|x\\|^2+\\|y\\|^2}{2\\sigma^2}\\right)\\exp\\!\\left(\\dfrac{x^Ty}{\\sigma^2}\\right)$", "$K_1(x,y)+K_2(x,y)$", "$K_1(x,y)-K_2(x,y)$"], "key": {"opts": [0, 1, 2]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> which combinations of $K_1$ and $K_2$ are valid kernels.</p>", "idea": "<p>Reduce each option to a sum of valid kernels, or find a failure (diagonal or Gram) for the odd one.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Option 1: $K_2(x,x)=1$, so the normalisation does nothing: it equals $K_2+x^Ty$, a sum of valid kernels. Valid.</li><li>Option 2: $\\exp(-\\tfrac{\\|x\\|^2+\\|y\\|^2}{2\\sigma^2})\\exp(\\tfrac{x^Ty}{\\sigma^2})=\\exp(-\\tfrac{\\|x-y\\|^2}{2\\sigma^2})=K_2$, because $-\\|x\\|^2-\\|y\\|^2+2x^Ty=-\\|x-y\\|^2$. So option 2 is $x^Ty+K_2$: valid.</li><li>Option 3: $K_1+K_2$: valid.</li><li>Option 4: $K_1-K_2$ at $x=y=0$ is $0-1=-1<0$, violating $k(x,x)\\ge0$. Invalid.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Subtraction is not always invalid ($3xy-xy$ is valid); here the diagonal check shows the failure.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code ($\\sigma=1$): options 1, 2, 3 give the same value $-0.93644$ at a random pair; 2000 random Grams: options 1 to 3 never fail, option 4 fails each time; $K_1(0,0)-K_2(0,0)=-1$ for every $\\sigma$.</p>", "trap": "Saying \"subtraction is never valid\" as a rule instead of checking, or missing that option 2 is the Gaussian in disguise.", "rule": "<p>Rewrite exponential forms, normalise by $k(x,x)$, then use closure; test the diagonal for subtractions.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "With $\\sigma=1$ is $K_2(x,y)-0.5$ a valid kernel?", "a": "Invalid. Pairs pass Cauchy–Schwarz, but for three far-apart points ($K_2\\approx0$) the Gram matrix is about $-0.5$ off the diagonal and $0.5$ on it with a negative eigenvalue."}, "optWhy": ["<b>Valid.</b>", "<b>Valid.</b>", "<b>Valid.</b>", "Invalid: $k(0,0)=-1$."]}}, {"id": "kr18", "exam": "Jul 2023", "year": "2023", "qno": "Q6", "marks": 5, "type": "MSQ", "concept": "kpca", "tag": "DERIVED", "sub": "3.4 Kernel PCA", "tagNote": "The source marks no correct option. The key (2 and 3) is derived [?].", "wording": "Which of the following statements about kernel PCA is/are always true?", "options": ["The number of principal components corresponding to nonzero eigenvalues obtained using kernel PCA on a $d$ dimensional dataset cannot be more than $d$.", "For every transformation mapping $\\phi:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\mathbb R^D$, there exists a valid kernel function.", "For two valid kernels $k_1,k_2:\\mathbb R^d\\times\\mathbb R^d\\to\\mathbb R$, $ak_1+bk_2$ is a valid kernel function. Here both $a$ and $b$ are positive real numbers.", "The output of a valid kernel function can never be a negative real number."], "key": {"opts": [1, 2]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> which statements are always true.</p>", "idea": "<p>Every feature map $\\phi$ defines a valid kernel $k=\\phi^T\\phi$; positive combinations of valid kernels are valid; kernel PCA can give more than $d$ non-zero components; and kernel values can be negative.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Option 1 (non-zero components cannot exceed $d$): false. The feature space can have dimension (or rank) above $d$; the limit is $\\min(D_{\\text{eff}},n-1)$. Example: $k=\\cos(x-y)$ in $d=1$ gives $2$ non-zero components.</li><li>Option 2 (every $\\phi$ has a valid kernel): true, since $k(x,y)=\\phi(x)^T\\phi(y)$ has a PSD Gram matrix by construction.</li><li>Option 3 ($ak_1+bk_2$ with $a,b>0$): true (closure under non-negative combinations).</li><li>Option 4 (a valid kernel is never negative): false. $x^Ty$ takes negative values (dot products can be negative); only $k(x,x)\\ge0$ is required.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>The limit for kernel PCA components is $\\min(D_{\\text{eff}},n-1)$ (feature-space rank and one less than the number of points because of centring).</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: the $\\cos(x-y)$ Gram matrix is PSD (minimum eigenvalue about $8\\times10^{-17}$); centred Gram eigenvalues $\\{4,0.667,0\\}$ give $2$ non-zero components with $d=1$.</p>", "trap": "Carrying over \"PCA gives at most $d$ components\" to kernel PCA, or rejecting option 2 because \"some $\\phi$ look odd\".", "rule": "<p>Kernel PCA components $\\le\\min(D_{\\text{eff}},n-1)$; kernel values can be negative; $k$ defined as $\\phi^T\\phi$ is always valid.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Gaussian kernel with $n=10$ generic points in $\\mathbb R^2$: maximum non-zero components? And for the linear kernel on the same data?", "a": "Gaussian: $\\min(\\infty,9)=9$. Linear: $\\min(2,9)=2$."}, "optWhy": ["False: kernel PCA can have more than $d$ components.", "<b>True.</b>", "<b>True.</b>", "False: $k(x,y)$ can be negative for $x\\ne y$."]}}, {"id": "kr19", "exam": "Feb 2024", "year": "2024", "qno": "Q5", "marks": 4, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "kpca", "tag": "DERIVED", "sub": "3.4 Kernel PCA", "tagNote": "The source marks no correct option. The key (option 3) is derived [?].", "wording": "Consider a dataset consisting of 1000 samples in a 20-dimensional space. You apply Kernel PCA with a polynomial kernel of degree 3 to reduce the dimensionality of the data. Which of the following statements regarding Kernel PCA is true?", "options": ["Kernel PCA always results in a lower-dimensional representation of the data compared to standard PCA.", "Kernel PCA can only be applied to datasets that are linearly separable.", "Choosing a higher degree polynomial kernel in Kernel PCA can lead to increased flexibility in capturing non-linear relationships.", "None of these."], "key": {"opts": [2]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the true statement about kernel PCA with a degree-3 polynomial kernel.</p>", "idea": "<p>The polynomial feature space is much larger than $\\mathbb R^{20}$, and higher degree adds more monomials, hence more flexibility.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Option 1 (always lower-dimensional than standard PCA): false. Standard PCA has at most $20$ components; kernel PCA here can give up to $\\min(\\binom{23}3-1,999)=999$ non-zero components. You choose how many to keep.</li><li>Option 2 (only linearly separable data): false. Kernel PCA is unsupervised and is used for non-linear structure.</li><li>Option 3 (higher degree gives more flexibility): true; more monomials are available.</li><li>Option 4 (None of these): false since option 3 is true.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Counts: $(1+x^Ty)^3$ on $\\mathbb R^{20}$ has $\\binom{23}3=1771$ features; $(x^Ty)^3$ has $\\binom{22}3=1540$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: for $n=1000$ generic points in $\\mathbb R^{20}$ the centred Gram rank is $999$ for both polynomial forms; standard PCA rank is $20$.</p>", "trap": "Hearing \"dimensionality reduction\" and assuming fewer dimensions always, or linking kernel PCA to separability (a classification idea).", "rule": "<p>Kernel PCA: bigger intermediate space, you pick how many PCs; higher degree gives more flexibility.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "$d=5$, $n=200$, $k=(1+x^Ty)^2$: max non-zero components? And for $(x^Ty)^2$?", "a": "$\\binom72-1=20$ (below $n-1=199$): $20$. Homogeneous: $\\binom62=15$. Both exceed $d=5$."}, "optWhy": ["False: the feature space grows, and how many PCs you keep is your choice.", "False: separability is not required.", "<b>True.</b>", "False."]}}, {"id": "kr20", "exam": "Jul 2024 (07 Jul)", "year": "2024", "qno": "Q5", "marks": 3, "type": "SA", "concept": "kpca", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "3.4 Kernel PCA", "wording": "Kernel PCA with a polynomial kernel of degree 3 is run on a dataset of 100 data-points. Let $\\phi$ be the corresponding transformation. If the largest eigenvalue of the centered kernel matrix is 10, find the largest eigenvalue of the covariance matrix of the transformed dataset.", "key": {"lo": 0.1, "hi": 0.1, "show": "0.1"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> $\\lambda_{\\max}(C_\\phi)$ in terms of the eigenvalue of the centred Gram matrix.</p>", "idea": "<p>The covariance of the (centred) transformed data is $C=\\tfrac1n\\tilde\\Phi\\tilde\\Phi^T$ and the centred Gram matrix is $K^c=\\tilde\\Phi^T\\tilde\\Phi$. They share non-zero eigenvalues up to the factor $n$: $\\lambda_C=\\lambda_{K^c}/n$.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>$\\lambda_C=\\tfrac{10}{100}=0.1$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>With $\\tfrac1{n-1}$ you would get $10/99=0.101$, which does not match the key; the course uses $\\tfrac1n$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code on a small example: eigenvalues of $\\tfrac13\\tilde\\Phi\\tilde\\Phi^T$ equal those of $K^c$ divided by $3$.</p>", "trap": "Answering $10$ (forgetting to divide by $n$).", "rule": "<p>Gram eigenvalue $/\\,n$ = PC variance (kernel PCA, dual PCA alike).</p>", "transfer": {"q": "$n=50$, top centred-Gram eigenvalue $35$, $\\operatorname{tr}K^c=70$. PC1 variance and explained variance ratio?", "a": "$35/50=0.7$; EVR $=35/70=50\\%$ ($n$ cancels)."}}}, {"id": "kr21", "exam": "Oct 2024 (27 Oct)", "year": "2024", "qno": "Q4", "marks": 3, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "kpca", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "3.4 Kernel PCA", "wording": "Given $n$ data points in a $d$-dimensional space with a non-linear relationship, kernel PCA is applied to reduce dimensionality and select the first $k$ principal components. Is it possible for $k$ to be greater than $d$?", "options": ["yes", "No"], "key": {"opts": [0]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> whether the number of kernel PCA components can exceed $d$.</p>", "idea": "<p>The limit is set by the feature-space rank and $n-1$, not by $d$.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Kernel PCA works in the feature space of $\\phi$, which can have dimension above $d$ (even infinite for the Gaussian kernel).</li><li>So up to $\\min(D_{\\text{eff}},n-1)$ non-zero components exist; this can exceed $d$. Yes.</li><li>Caution: a linear kernel gives at most $\\min(d,n-1)$, but the question is about a non-linear relationship.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>\"Reduce dimensionality\" means keeping fewer components than the feature space has, not fewer than $d$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: the explicit feature covariance for $k=\\cos(x-y)$, $d=1$, has two non-zero eigenvalues; a linear kernel in $d=2$ with $n=10$ gives rank $2$.</p>", "trap": "Assuming $k\\le d$ from the name \"dimensionality reduction\".", "rule": "<p>Kernel PCA components $\\le\\min(D_{\\text{eff}},n-1)$; for the linear kernel $\\min(d,n-1)$.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "(a) Linear kernel, $d=3$: maximum non-zero components? (b) Any kernel, $n=2$ points?", "a": "(a) at most $3$. (b) at most $n-1=1$."}, "optWhy": ["<b>Correct.</b>", "No would hold only for the linear kernel."]}}, {"id": "kr22", "exam": "Jul 2025 (13 Jul)", "year": "2025", "qno": "Q5", "marks": 4, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "kpca", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "3.4 Kernel PCA", "context": "Data matrix $X=\\begin{bmatrix}1&0\\\\0&1\\\\0&0\\end{bmatrix}$ (columns are the points $x_1=(1,0,0)^T$, $x_2=(0,1,0)^T$, so $n=2$); kernel $k(x_i,x_j)=(1+x_i^Tx_j)^2$. Centring: $k^c_{ij}=k_{ij}-\\theta_i-\\theta_j+P$ with $\\theta_i=\\tfrac1n\\sum_kk_{ik}$ and $P=\\tfrac1{n^2}\\sum_{ij}k_{ij}$.", "wording": "Kernel PCA with a polynomial kernel is applied to the dataset with three features given above. Find the centered kernel matrix $K^c$.", "options": ["$\\begin{bmatrix}-1.5&1.5\\\\1.5&-1.5\\end{bmatrix}$", "$\\begin{bmatrix}1.5&-1.5\\\\-1.5&1.5\\end{bmatrix}$", "$\\begin{bmatrix}4&1\\\\1&4\\end{bmatrix}$", "$\\begin{bmatrix}1&-1\\\\4&-4\\end{bmatrix}$"], "key": {"opts": [1]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> $K^c$ for the two points.</p>", "idea": "<p>First the Gram matrix $K$, then centre it: $K^c=HKH$, i.e. subtract row means and column means and add the grand mean.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>$x_1^Tx_1=1$, $x_2^Tx_2=1$, $x_1^Tx_2=0$. So $K=\\begin{bmatrix}(1+1)^2&(1+0)^2\\\\(1+0)^2&(1+1)^2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4&1\\\\1&4\\end{bmatrix}$.</li><li>Row means $\\theta=(2.5,2.5)$; grand mean $P=2.5$.</li><li>$k^c_{11}=4-2.5-2.5+2.5=1.5$; $k^c_{12}=1-2.5-2.5+2.5=-1.5$. So $K^c=\\begin{bmatrix}1.5&-1.5\\\\-1.5&1.5\\end{bmatrix}$ (option 2).</li><li>Quick property checks: row sums must be $0$; the matrix is PSD (eigenvalues $3,0$).</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Forgetting the $+P$ term gives $4-2.5-2.5=-1$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: $HKH$ and the elementwise formula agree; $K^c\\mathbf 1=0$; eigenvalues $3,0$; trace $3$.</p>", "trap": "Answering with $K$ itself, or omitting $+P$.", "rule": "<p>A centred Gram matrix is symmetric, has zero row sums, a non-negative diagonal, and is PSD.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "$K=\\begin{bmatrix}2&1&0\\\\1&2&1\\\\0&1&2\\end{bmatrix}$. Find $K^c_{12}$.", "a": "$\\theta=(1,\\tfrac43,1)$, $P=\\tfrac{10}9$: $K^c_{12}=1-1-\\tfrac43+\\tfrac{10}9=-\\tfrac29\\approx-0.222$."}, "optWhy": ["Signs reversed: the diagonal of a centred Gram matrix must be $\\ge0$.", "<b>Correct.</b> Rows sum to $0$, PSD.", "This is $K$, uncentred.", "Not symmetric."]}}], "test": [{"id": "t1", "concept": "poly", "level": "easy", "type": "num", "stem": "Number of features of $(1+x^Ty)^3$ when $x\\in\\mathbb R^5$?", "num": {"ans": 56, "tol": 0.001, "show": "56"}, "explain": "$\\binom{5+3}{3}=\\binom83=56$.", "hint": ""}, {"id": "t2", "concept": "poly", "level": "easy", "type": "num", "stem": "$k(x,y)=(x^Ty+1)^3$, $x=(1,2)$, $y=(2,0)$. Value?", "num": {"ans": 27, "tol": 0.001, "show": "27"}, "explain": "$x^Ty=1\\cdot2+2\\cdot0=2$; $2+1=3$; $3^3=27$.", "hint": ""}, {"id": "t3", "concept": "gauss", "level": "easy", "type": "num", "stem": "Gaussian kernel, $\\sigma=2$, $x=(0,0)$, $y=(2,0)$. Value to 3 decimals?", "num": {"ans": 0.607, "tol": 0.002, "show": "0.607"}, "explain": "$\\|x-y\\|^2=4$, $2\\sigma^2=8$, $e^{-0.5}=0.607$.", "hint": ""}, {"id": "t4", "concept": "valid", "level": "medium", "type": "mcq", "stem": "Which is NOT a valid kernel?", "options": [{"t": "$x^Ty+1$", "ok": false, "why": "Valid: linear kernel plus a non-negative constant."}, {"t": "$\\|x-y\\|$", "ok": true, "why": "Invalid: Gram matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}0&a\\\\a&0\\end{bmatrix}$ has eigenvalue $-a$."}, {"t": "$e^{-\\|x-y\\|^2}$", "ok": false, "why": "Valid: Gaussian."}, {"t": "$3(x^Ty)^2+2$", "ok": false, "why": "Valid: non-negative combination of a squared valid kernel and a constant."}]}, {"id": "t5", "concept": "phi", "level": "medium", "type": "mcq", "stem": "A feature map for $k(x,y)=(x^Ty)^2$ on $\\mathbb R^2$ is", "options": [{"t": "$(x_1^2,x_2^2,x_1x_2)$", "ok": false, "why": "The cross term gives $x_1x_2y_1y_2$, not $2x_1x_2y_1y_2$."}, {"t": "$(x_1^2,x_2^2,\\sqrt2x_1x_2)$", "ok": true, "why": "Correct: $\\sqrt2\\cdot\\sqrt2=2$."}, {"t": "$(x_1,x_2,x_1x_2)$", "ok": false, "why": "Wrong degrees."}, {"t": "$(x_1^2,x_2^2,2x_1x_2)$", "ok": false, "why": "The cross term would be $4x_1x_2y_1y_2$."}]}, {"id": "t6", "concept": "kpca", "level": "medium", "type": "num", "stem": "Centred Gram matrix has top eigenvalue $24$, $n=60$. Largest eigenvalue of the covariance matrix of the transformed data?", "num": {"ans": 0.4, "tol": 0.001, "show": "0.4"}, "explain": "$24/60=0.4$.", "hint": ""}, {"id": "t7", "concept": "kpca", "level": "hard", "type": "mcq", "stem": "Kernel PCA, Gaussian kernel, $n=10$ generic points in $\\mathbb R^2$. Maximum non-zero components?", "options": [{"t": "2", "ok": false, "why": "That is the linear kernel limit $\\min(d,n-1)$."}, {"t": "9", "ok": true, "why": "Correct: $\\min(\\infty,n-1)=9$."}, {"t": "10", "ok": false, "why": "Centring removes one dimension."}, {"t": "Infinite", "ok": false, "why": "Cannot exceed $n-1$."}]}, {"id": "t8", "concept": "kpca", "level": "hard", "type": "num", "stem": "$K=\\begin{bmatrix}2&1&0\\\\1&2&1\\\\0&1&2\\end{bmatrix}$. Centred entry $K^c_{12}$ to 3 decimals?", "num": {"ans": -0.222, "tol": 0.002, "show": "-0.222"}, "explain": "$\\theta=(1,4/3,1)$, $P=10/9$; $1-1-4/3+10/9=-2/9$.", "hint": ""}, {"id": "t9", "concept": "valid", "level": "hard", "type": "mcq", "stem": "$K_2$ is Gaussian. Is $K_2(x,y)-0.5$ a valid kernel?", "options": [{"t": "Yes, subtracting a constant keeps PSD", "ok": false, "why": "Subtraction of a positive constant is not safe."}, {"t": "No: far-apart points give a Gram matrix with a negative eigenvalue", "ok": true, "why": "Correct: for 3 far-apart points the Gram matrix is about $0.5$ on the diagonal and $-0.5$ off it, with a negative eigenvalue."}, {"t": "Yes, because $K_2\\le1$", "ok": false, "why": "The bound on values is irrelevant."}, {"t": "Cannot be decided", "ok": false, "why": "It can be decided with a Gram matrix."}]}, {"id": "t10", "concept": "gauss", "level": "hard", "type": "num", "stem": "Squared feature-space distance for two points with Gaussian kernel value $k(x,y)=0.6$?", "num": {"ans": 0.8, "tol": 0.001, "show": "0.8"}, "explain": "$k(x,x)+k(y,y)-2k(x,y)=1+1-1.2=0.8$.", "hint": ""}], "checklist": [{"id": "c1", "t": "I can read a feature map off a kernel by splitting x-part and y-part."}, {"id": "c2", "t": "I know the tests for a valid kernel and the closure rules."}, {"id": "c3", "t": "I can centre a Gram matrix and convert its eigenvalue to a PC variance (divide by n)."}, {"id": "c4", "t": "I know kernel PCA can give more than d components (up to min(D_eff, n-1))."}], "formulas": [{"name": "Kernel", "tex": "k(x,y)=\\phi(x)^T\\phi(y)", "note": "Valid iff Gram matrix is symmetric PSD."}, {"name": "Polynomial", "tex": "(1+x^Ty)^m", "note": "$\\binom{d+m}{m}$ features."}, {"name": "Gaussian", "tex": "\\exp\\!\\Big(-\\frac{\\|x-y\\|^2}{2\\sigma^2}\\Big)", "note": "$k(x,x)=1$."}, {"name": "Centred Gram", "tex": "K^c=HKH,\\ H=I-\\tfrac1n\\mathbf 1\\mathbf 1^T", "note": "Row sums zero."}]};